1. BINDING CLARIFICATION of the dec 086 item 3 FINALS RULE. It applies to both tracks and replaces the "match" clause. A final must EITHER (a) have scored COMPLETE itself, OR (b) differ from a COMPLETE-scored bundle ONLY in eval_config/generation knobs that can't lengthen a task: cap <=, max_tool_calls <=, max_turns <=, thinking_budget <=, max_output_tokens <= for every agent. Its prompts, instructions, sub-agent structure, tools, skills and adapters must be byte-identical to that bundle's. A different prompt or lineage does NOT match, because the prompt changes how many tasks run to the cap, and the margin depends on that count. So the consequence line in dec 086 stands as intended. v012 (v006 lineage) does NOT match v002 and needs its own COMPLETE score before it can be Final A. v009 qualifies only through its own Nov 8 score. A COMPLETE v009 would then cover v009-lineage bundles with <= knobs, but not v012, because v012 has higher thinking on main.
2. Record this in STATE in place of the OPEN line, and make the finals-eligibility check in the lb json / finals checklist mechanical: zip-diff vs the reference bundle limited to eval_config/generation knob decreases.
3. Slot planning: if report 021 shows arm 3 (v012) winning, propose a v012 scored submission in that report for the first free slot after e4b-v002h (Nov 9). Slots are plentiful (~28); the timeout risk on Final A is not.
4. Nothing else changes: Nov 8 v009 checklist, e4b-v002h Nov 9, report 021 on arm-3 land.

Reasoning: the hard constraint is never losing a submission to a timeout. Under H3 the theoretical worst case exceeds 12 h, so only scored evidence covers a bundle, and that evidence transfers only when every task can only get shorter. A prompt change can't be shown to do that. A spare slot is cheap compared with the risk on Final A. The rule is locked before arm-3 data exists, so the arm-3 result can't bias it.
